      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path:</strong> The fire pause breaks (F2 YES) and US extends its strike campaign; Hormuz remains frozen through October with no insurance re-entry and no diplomatic off-ramp, as the F4 trajectory at 7% recovery probability projects.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path:</strong> Both sides sustain the current equilibrium — intermittent strikes, Hormuz at 10–20% of pre-war capacity, and UNGA contacts produce process without a held session (F3 NO), with the conflict extending into November at current intensity.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path:</strong> A held UNGA proximity session (F3 YES) produces a Doha-framework ceasefire; the fire pause formalizes, Hormuz transit ban lifts, and traffic recovers toward the 30/day threshold before October 3 (F4 YES).</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN (descriptive, ~200 words) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · ~200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iranian state media treated UNGA attendance as a sovereignty assertion rather than diplomatic outreach this week. IRNA's September 20 reporting carried the phrase that attendance is Iran's "legal right" without diplomatic framing language; Kayhan ran coverage of a government-organized demonstration in Tehran alongside the announcement that over 600,000 Iranians enrolled in a government-backed military training initiative. The dominant editorial line in hardline outlets was resistance and national endurance, not negotiation readiness. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>Iran's judiciary on September 19 confirmed the execution of a man convicted of transmitting intelligence on Iranian missile sites to Israel during the February–March 2026 campaign, per the Times of Israel liveblog. State media presented the execution as a wartime national-security enforcement action; no named Iranian official linked it to any deterrence signal directed at the United States or Israel. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>Mohsen Rezaei's transmission of Iranian conditions for ending the war through the Doha channel on September 19 (Al Jazeera liveblog) generated split coverage inside Iran. Reformist-aligned outlets treated the conditions relay as a diplomatic opening; Kayhan and hardline media described the same event as Tehran setting terms from strength rather than seeking an exit. The Iranian Foreign Ministry made no separate public statement confirming or elaborating on the conditions as of September 20. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

